`inventory-dispositioned.json` is written (81 rows, validated against the output contract: one disposition and one class each, `barrier` present on and only on the 52 excluded rows).

**The pipeline STOPs. Three characteristic-core rows exit: A2, A8, A9.**

All three are the opcode wire encoding: A2 (byte-order care on `ar$op`), A8 (`REQUEST = 1`, high byte first), A9 (`REPLY = 2`). Their obligations fix numerals and byte order. Scope section 2 removes both (A-PARSE hands the core a decoded record; `Opcode` is the two-element enum; section 6 has no arithmetic), and section 2's exclusion table names "high-byte-first encoding of `ar$hrd`, `ar$pro`, `ar$op`" as OUT in advance, with pre-commitment 2 forbidding recovery. So the conflict is between the core list and the A-PARSE boundary, both fixed before this stage. I did not re-grade them: each reason says so, and each explicitly refuses the B7 escape, since "REQUEST and REPLY are distinct elements of a declared sort" is the weakened surrogate, not the row's obligation. The interop content is exactly what makes the rows core.

Counts: 25 `Encoded`, 4 `Deployment-modeled` (A41 ar$tha havoc, A47/A49 M-OPTCHECK, A62 uninterpreted `HwType`), 0 `Vectored`, 52 out (B5 22, B7 12, B2 9, B8 8, B1 1). 18 of the 21 core rows are `Encoded`.

Three things worth your attention:

- **A61 was the near-miss.** "Merged into the table before the opcode is looked at" reads as trace-level (B3), and B3 would have halted a fourth core row. It is encoded instead as a non-dependence: the merge and add clauses' guards do not mention `ar$op`, so two packets differing only in opcode produce the same table post-state. That is a single-transition property and it is the clause P-SPOOF rests on.
- **B8 is stretched on the monitor rows** (A72, A73, A75, A76, A77, A79, A81). Their exclusion ground is R4, the monitor is a principal the scope refuses, which is not a listed barrier. I read B8 "outside any tool" as "no encoder, model, or check block in this pipeline reaches it" and said so in each reason. On a strict reading ("no tool anywhere could verify it") that is false for A73 and A77, which are plainly tool-checkable, and those rows would be a second STOP for citing no listed barrier. R4 justifies them in writing, and the gate exists to catch exclusions nobody can justify, so I did not halt on them. If your reading of B8 is the strict one, this is where the ledger needs a ninth barrier. A74 and A80 (parsing) went to B5 and A78 (endless loop) to B1 on their own merits, independent of R4.
- **No row is `Vectored`, and no row is C5.** The scope commits exactly one executed check, the lines 367-410 trace, and the inventory extracts no row from that range because it is not normative. The trace is an exhibition obligation on the model; every row it touches is already carried by a contract clause. C2 and C4 are also unused: section 3 removes arithmetic, and the protocol has no opaque primitive.
